Simon -

Base data structures for gathering data from each user, as well as creating a database of plants as they get indentified. Also connected Wikipedia to our project to give descriptions about each plant. 

- create.sql: wrote the 5 database tables: users (login info + points), plants (common name, scientific name, description), users_to_plants (which plants each user found, with photo, location, and date), badges (name, description, number of plants required), and users_to_badges (which badges each user earned). Used foreign keys with ON DELETE CASCADE so deleting a user removes their data.
  - insert.sql: added starter data: 6 badges (from 1 to 500 plants found) and 5 Colorado starter plants.
  - plantHelpers.js: added getPlantDescription(), which looks up a plant's scientific name on Wikipedia and returns a 1–2 sentence description (with a fallback message if no page exists), and getOrCreatePlant(), which adds a plant to the database the first time anyone identifies it, so Wikipedia is only called once per species.
  - index.js (collection-routes branch, not merged yet): added GET /collection, which lists the logged-in user's plants newest first, and GET /plants/:id, which shows one plant's details (name, scientific name, description, photo, date first found); users can only view plants they've found.

Nicholas - Working prototype for plant identification from a photo, plus a Handlebars page framework for the app's frontend.

Added

Working identification prototype: takes a JPG or PNG image input and finds the closest matching plant, then outputs a parsed list including confidence, scientific name, and standard/common names.
API route: POST /api/identify, multipart form-data, field image, optional organ (leaf/flower/fruit/bark/auto, default auto).
Parsed output fields: bestMatch, scientificName, commonName, commonNames, genus, family, confidence, confidencePercent, predictedOrgan, alternatives, and remainingIdentificationRequests — replacing the raw Pl@ntNet JSON response, which was harder to use on a page.
Verified end-to-end on Oct 31: a photo returned Blue fescue, Festuca glauca Vill., at 93.9% confidence, predicted organ leaf, via the JSON identify route.
Handlebars framework: a shared layout and partials structure (main layout, header, footer) with separate pages for home/camera, login, register, search/Bloomdex, plant detail, profile, and settings, plus a shared stylesheet and script file. Pages can be tweaked through the layout and partials instead of wildly changing large amounts of code per page.

